onboarding-chain edges into E009.** E009's scope states it \"pull[s] upstream manifests from the midstream fork at pinned commits via `get_all_manifests.sh`\" — an artifact dependency on the fork that E002 establishes and pins (E002's scope explicitly pins \"the commit(s) the platform integration will render manifests from,\" naming E009's consumption). Yet E009's only dependency is E001, and it has neither an edge to E002 nor a manifest-source/pin AC. Rule 6 makes repo-onboarding → general implementation of the onboarded component always serial (\"code needs repo\"); Rule 13 makes an epic whose code builds against another epic's artifact a serial consumer; Rule 9 makes license-validation → general implementation serial (license findings change the dependencies and manifests being integrated); and Rule 20's onboarding chain places the general implementation after license-validation, parallel only with konflux-onboarding. E009 is the kagenti-operator component's general implementation in this plan — the decomposition summary's claim that \"there is no general implementation epic\" for these repos conflates the absent runtime-feature work (genuinely out of scope, RHAIRFE-7587/7588) with the platform-integration work that consumes the onboarded repo. Fix: add E002 → E009 and E003 → E009 edges while preserving E004 ∥ E009 parallelism (Rule 10).\n66\t\n67\tMinor — E005 → E008 serializes the committed authbridge-proxy Konflux onboarding behind a PM sign-off (E005), in tension with the strategy's top-risk mitigation (\"submit Konflux onboarding request immediately\"); authbridge-proxy is the committed TP minimum per the P1 HLR regardless of the variant decision, so gating only the extended variant set via an AC (as done for digests on E010) would preserve the parallel start.\n68\t\n69\tMinor — E012's feature-flag ACs gate UI visibility on the DSC kagenti-operator component state that E009 defines, with no edge or AC making that coupling explicit; the parallelism is defensible (the flag can default-hide), but an AC noting verification against a live Managed/Removed component once E009 lands would make the intended ordering explicit.\n70\t\n71\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (0/2)\n72\t\n73\t**Major — E009 is clearly oversized.** Its scope bundles the KagentiOperator CRD definition, the DSC schema extension, the controller action pipeline with registry integration, kustomize manifest integration with RHOAI-specific overlays, and the Removed-state garbage-collection/finalizer lifecycle including cross-namespace cleanup semantics. The strategy's own effort estimate assigns 2–3 sprints to exactly this bucket (\"rhods-operator component controller (2-3 sprints)\"), which exceeds the ~2-week epic guideline by the rubric's \"multiple sprints\" definition — while the comparable Konflux bucket (2–3 sprints) was split into E004+E008 and the dashboard bucket (1–2 sprints) into E012+E013. Split E009 along the pattern's natural seams (e.g., CRD + DSC schema + controller core vs. kustomize manifest integration + cleanup lifecycle) or record an explicit sizing justification.\n74\t\n75\tOtherwise clean: every epic has a single component/team tuple; the same-team Agentic epics are correctly separated by repo and work product (E002/E006 distinct forks, E003/E007 distinct scan targets, E004/E008 distinct image families); E012/E013 split along the Rule 2 architectural axis (Go BFF backend vs. React federated UI) with no scope duplication — E013 consumes the API E012 produces; E014's test-plan-plus-E2E bundling is intrinsic QE work, not code/content mixing; E016 is the separate docs-authoring epic. No epic other than E009 appears to exceed a sprint.\n76\t\n77\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n78\t\n79\tAll four Investigations (E001, E005, E011, E015) resolve genuine uncertainty that changes downstream scope or existence, and each has downstream dependents (E001 → E009 + branch B; E005 → E008; E011 → E013; E015 → E016). All Implementation epics produce concrete artifacts. Gating metadata is complete and correct: every epic with `gated_by` has `gate_failure_impact` populated with both `action` and a real `fallback_approach` (no scheduling-only pseudo-gates); every `gated_by` target appears in that epic's direct `dependencies` (E008→E005, E009→E001, E013→E011, E016→E015, branch→E001); no epic lists an Investigation dependency without `gated_by`. The deliberate non-gating choices are justified: E001 does not gate E002/E004 (Konflux submission is procedural and the strategy's top risk demands immediate start), and E011 does not gate E012 (the BFF API surface is invariant across the depth decision).\n80\t\n81\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n82\t\n83\tType-appropriate signal fields throughout: all 12 Implementation epics (including the branch epic) carry `ai_signals`; all 4 Investigations carry `investigation_signals`; no epic mixes or omits the correct set. All 17 ai-signals rationale files exist and their tabulated values match the frontmatter. Spot-checks held up against the rubric and strategy content: E009 `open_questions: -1` (gated on the gap analysis); E008/E013 `change_specificity: 0` (scope pending the variant/depth decisions); E005/E011/E015 `human_judgment_required: -2` with `source_accessibility: 0` (PM/UX decisions not yet in any artifact); E001 `source_accessibility: 1` (answer readable in the open upstream repos); E004 `external_dependency`/`human_process_gates: -1` (Konflux lead time, needs submission); E014 `existing_foundation: 0` (new E2E suite). E002/E003/E006/E007 `open_questions: 1` is defensible — deterministic process work whose only uncertainty is what the epic itself resolves. Cosmetic nit only: BRANCH-B-E017-ai-signals.md repeats its header line twice.\n84\t\n85\t### 6. Acceptance Criteria Quality (2/2)\n86\t\n87\tEvery epic has testable acceptance criteria, largely Given/When/Then with measured-by clauses lifted from the strategy (including the corrected `agentruntimes.agent.kagenti.dev` CRD name — the strategy's `agentrimes` typo was rightly fixed and flagged in the summary). Rule-mandated ACs verified across all epics: E016 (docs-authoring) has the technical-review-against-implementation AC (Rule 24); E004 and E008 each have build-pipeline-green and license-scan ACs (Rule 25); E009's \"Konflux-built image\" AC gates first execution on the pipeline (Rule 25 via its dependency chain); E010 has the pinned-digests-available AC (Rule 13 configuration-reference exception); Rule 23 (rollback/feature-flag) does not apply — no epic replaces an existing capability, and the new-component default-Removed posture is asserted as an E009 AC. No epic body references sibling epics by bare draft ID (grep-verified across all 17 files).\n88\t\n89\t### 7. Completeness (2/2)\n90\t\n91\tFull scope coverage: all four affected components' work is present (Konflux onboarding E002–E008, component controller E009, CSV packaging E010, dashboard E011–E013), plus QE (E014) and docs (E016). Every strategy open question is either an Investigation (E001, E005, E011, E015) or explicitly dispositioned in the summary with reasoning (cross-namespace cleanup as an E009 AC/finalizer design detail; Keycloak folded into E001's validation scope; image contract documented by E016 rather than decided; sandbox interaction out of scope). All seven proposed Given/When/Then ACs are allocated and present in epic bodies (E009: 1–3; E004: 4; E008: 5; E013: 6; E010+E014: 7). All five strategy risks are carried forward as epic scope or ACs (Konflux deadline → E004 immediate submission; upstream gaps → E001 + branch B; webhook ordering → E014; orphaned resources → E009/E014; BFF port collision → E012 AC). Documentation Support is required (MUST documentation AC) and the E016 docs-authoring epic exists. Branch B covers the bounded gap outcome with the >2-sprint scope-reduction path captured in `gate_failure_impact`; the E005 and E015 outcome spaces are handled as scope-flex/deferral rather than branches, wi